1
00:00:00,333 --> 00:00:01,931
Nineteen seventy-eight.

2
00:00:01,931 --> 00:00:05,752
A twenty-year-old pilot is alone over the sea at night.

3
00:00:05,752 --> 00:00:11,866
He tells the controller there is a huge shape above him, and that it is not an aircraft.

4
00:00:12,333 --> 00:00:14,778
The controller records everything.

5
00:00:14,778 --> 00:00:19,524
Then comes seventeen seconds of a strange metallic scraping noise.

6
00:00:19,524 --> 00:00:20,818
And then, silence.

7
00:00:20,818 --> 00:00:23,766
The pilot and his plane were never found.

8
00:00:24,233 --> 00:00:26,687
Was it a visitor from another world?

9
00:00:26,687 --> 00:00:28,392
A pilot lost in the dark?

10
00:00:28,392 --> 00:00:30,574
Or something stranger than both?

11
00:00:30,574 --> 00:00:33,233
Tonight, we replay the flight recorder.

12
00:00:33,700 --> 00:00:36,344
We follow the minutes as they happened.

13
00:00:36,344 --> 00:00:39,327
We set the evidence beside each explanation.

14
00:00:39,327 --> 00:00:41,633
And at the end, we give a verdict.

15
00:00:42,100 --> 00:00:45,447
The place is Bass Strait, off southern Australia.

16
00:00:45,447 --> 00:00:48,180
The date is the twenty-first of October.

17
00:00:48,180 --> 00:00:49,000
Let's begin.

18
00:00:53,466 --> 00:00:54,090
Test one.

19
00:00:54,090 --> 00:00:54,783
The pilot.

20
00:00:54,783 --> 00:00:58,732
Frederick Valentich was twenty years old, from Melbourne.

21
00:00:58,732 --> 00:01:02,266
He loved flying, and he wanted to fly for a living.

22
00:01:02,733 --> 00:01:07,755
He held a private licence, and in May of that year he gained a night rating.

23
00:01:07,755 --> 00:01:11,786
He had not yet passed all the exams for a commercial licence.

24
00:01:11,786 --> 00:01:14,033
He had failed some of them, twice.

25
00:01:14,500 --> 00:01:17,687
Friends described a careful, keen young man.

26
00:01:17,687 --> 00:01:23,410
He was also interested in unidentified flying objects, and had read about them.

27
00:01:23,410 --> 00:01:27,466
Investigators later noted that interest in their report.

28
00:01:27,933 --> 00:01:30,343
This matters, and it cuts both ways.

29
00:01:30,343 --> 00:01:32,820
It does not mean he invented a story.

30
00:01:32,820 --> 00:01:35,966
But it may have shaped how he read what he saw.

31
00:01:36,433 --> 00:01:41,280
He was low on night experience, and the sea at night has no horizon.

32
00:01:41,280 --> 00:01:42,564
Keep that in mind.

33
00:01:42,564 --> 00:01:43,633
It will return.

34
00:01:44,099 --> 00:01:47,799
That evening, he planned a short training flight across the strait.

35
00:01:48,266 --> 00:01:48,888
Test two.

36
00:01:48,888 --> 00:01:49,854
The departure.

37
00:01:49,854 --> 00:01:56,966
A Cessna one eighty-two, rented from a flying school, filled with fuel for about five hours in the air.

38
00:01:57,433 --> 00:02:04,274
The plan: from Moorabbin airport in Melbourne, to King Island, about two hundred thirty kilometres away.

39
00:02:04,274 --> 00:02:07,299
He took off a little after six in the evening.

40
00:02:07,766 --> 00:02:10,972
Strangely, he gave different reasons for the trip.

41
00:02:10,972 --> 00:02:13,665
Some said he was going to collect friends.

42
00:02:13,665 --> 00:02:16,166
Others, that he wanted to buy crayfish.

43
00:02:16,633 --> 00:02:18,075
That is a small thing.

44
00:02:18,075 --> 00:02:22,533
But it is the first loose thread, and we will mark it as unverified.

45
00:02:23,000 --> 00:02:25,621
At seven, he reported over Cape Otway.

46
00:02:25,621 --> 00:02:30,037
The weather was good, with excellent visibility and light winds.

47
00:02:30,037 --> 00:02:32,866
Daylight would end within twenty minutes.

48
00:02:33,333 --> 00:02:34,733
Everything is normal.

49
00:02:34,733 --> 00:02:37,733
Now the clock reaches six minutes past seven.

50
00:02:38,199 --> 00:02:38,993
Test three.

51
00:02:38,993 --> 00:02:40,219
The transmission.

52
00:02:40,219 --> 00:02:45,123
At six minutes past seven, Valentich calls Melbourne flight service.

53
00:02:45,123 --> 00:02:49,666
He asks if there is any known traffic below five thousand feet.

54
00:02:50,133 --> 00:02:51,542
The controller checks.

55
00:02:51,542 --> 00:02:53,208
There is no known traffic.

56
00:02:53,208 --> 00:02:55,066
Nothing is expected near him.

57
00:02:55,533 --> 00:03:02,019
Valentich says a large aircraft is there, with four bright lights that look like landing lights.

58
00:03:02,019 --> 00:03:05,533
It has passed over him, about a thousand feet above.

59
00:03:06,000 --> 00:03:10,844
He says it is moving at high speed, and it seems to be playing with him.

60
00:03:10,844 --> 00:03:16,766
He guesses it could be going one hundred miles an hour, then tries again to describe it.

61
00:03:17,233 --> 00:03:21,031
The controller asks him to identify the aircraft type.

62
00:03:21,031 --> 00:03:22,790
Valentich says he cannot.

63
00:03:22,790 --> 00:03:26,800
It is a long shape, he says, shiny, and metallic-looking.

64
00:03:27,266 --> 00:03:33,512
Then he reports a green light, and something like a mist, and a pulsing, as it comes closer.

65
00:03:33,512 --> 00:03:36,500
This is the detail that puzzles pilots most.

66
00:03:36,966 --> 00:03:39,525
Then, his engine begins to run rough.

67
00:03:39,525 --> 00:03:46,233
He is flying at about four thousand five hundred feet, and he reports the strange craft overhead.

68
00:03:46,699 --> 00:03:50,394
He says it is hovering, and that it is not an aircraft.

69
00:03:50,394 --> 00:03:55,633
Those words are among the most famous in the history of unexplained sightings.

70
00:03:56,099 --> 00:03:59,731
The controller replies calmly, asking his intentions.

71
00:03:59,731 --> 00:04:05,966
Valentich says he plans to carry on to King Island, and that the object is above him again.

72
00:04:06,433 --> 00:04:11,818
His last intelligible words are something like: it is not an aircraft, it is.

73
00:04:11,818 --> 00:04:14,266
And then the microphone stays open.

74
00:04:14,733 --> 00:04:19,820
For seventeen seconds, the tape records a harsh metallic scraping sound.

75
00:04:19,820 --> 00:04:20,739
Then nothing.

76
00:04:20,739 --> 00:04:24,766
That is the last evidence we have of Frederick Valentich.

77
00:04:25,233 --> 00:04:27,315
The controller calls him again.

78
00:04:27,315 --> 00:04:28,591
There is no answer.

79
00:04:28,591 --> 00:04:34,366
Within minutes, the alert phase begins, and at half past seven, a full distress phase.

80
00:04:34,833 --> 00:04:42,399
Search aircraft take off through the night and the following days, including an air force Orion and civilian planes.

81
00:04:42,866 --> 00:04:44,260
Test three complete.

82
00:04:44,260 --> 00:04:46,699
A voice, a sound, and an empty sea.

83
00:04:47,166 --> 00:04:47,820
Test four.

84
00:04:47,820 --> 00:04:48,539
The search.

85
00:04:48,539 --> 00:04:55,600
For four days, aircraft and ships cover more than two thousand six hundred square kilometres of Bass Strait.

86
00:04:56,066 --> 00:04:57,694
They find no wreckage.

87
00:04:57,694 --> 00:05:00,579
No oil slick that can be linked to him.

88
00:05:00,579 --> 00:05:01,541
No life raft.

89
00:05:01,541 --> 00:05:02,133
No body.

90
00:05:02,600 --> 00:05:04,818
That is odd, but not impossible.

91
00:05:04,818 --> 00:05:12,166
Cold, deep water and strong currents can swallow a small aircraft, and the strait is known for its storms.

92
00:05:12,633 --> 00:05:18,622
In nineteen eighty-three, a piece of engine cowling washes ashore on Flinders Island.

93
00:05:18,622 --> 00:05:21,511
It might be from a Cessna one eighty-two.

94
00:05:21,511 --> 00:05:24,400
It was never proven to be from his plane.

95
00:05:24,866 --> 00:05:29,229
A naval laboratory said drift from the crash area was plausible.

96
00:05:29,229 --> 00:05:32,433
But a possible fragment is not a confirmed one.

97
00:05:32,899 --> 00:05:34,490
The sea kept its secret.

98
00:05:34,490 --> 00:05:38,333
So the official investigators had to decide what to write.

99
00:05:38,800 --> 00:05:39,514
Test five.

100
00:05:39,514 --> 00:05:40,944
The official report.

101
00:05:40,944 --> 00:05:48,666
Australian aviation authorities finish a summary after the search, released publicly in nineteen eighty-two.

102
00:05:49,133 --> 00:05:50,733
Its conclusion is short.

103
00:05:50,733 --> 00:05:54,533
The reason for the disappearance could not be determined.

104
00:05:54,533 --> 00:05:56,866
The pilot is presumed to have died.

105
00:05:57,333 --> 00:06:05,576
The report notes his limited night experience, his unusual route planning, and his stated interest in unidentified objects.

106
00:06:05,576 --> 00:06:10,199
It does not say a craft was there, and it does not say there was not.

107
00:06:10,666 --> 00:06:16,061
That honest phrase, cause undetermined, is the most important fact in this case.

108
00:06:16,061 --> 00:06:19,500
It is not a cover-up, and it is not a confirmation.

109
00:06:19,966 --> 00:06:22,050
Newspapers did not wait for it.

110
00:06:22,050 --> 00:06:28,100
They ran with the strange words from the tape, and the story spread worldwide within days.

111
00:06:28,566 --> 00:06:31,600
Now we weigh the explanations, one at a time.

112
00:06:32,066 --> 00:06:32,773
Test six.

113
00:06:32,773 --> 00:06:34,657
The pilot's explanation.

114
00:06:34,657 --> 00:06:36,462
Spatial disorientation.

115
00:06:36,462 --> 00:06:42,899
Over open water on a dark night, with no horizon, the inner ear cannot be trusted.

116
00:06:43,366 --> 00:06:45,632
Pilots call it a black hole effect.

117
00:06:45,632 --> 00:06:50,100
The sea and the sky merge, and bright lights can seem to be anywhere.

118
00:06:50,566 --> 00:06:55,603
Two researchers, Richard Haines and Gregory Norman, studied the recording.

119
00:06:55,603 --> 00:07:03,500
They suggested he may have turned inverted without noticing, and mistaken ground or sky lights for another aircraft.

120
00:07:03,966 --> 00:07:12,733
Others noted that bright planets and stars were low in the sky that evening, and can look like moving lights when you are tilted and tired.

121
00:07:13,199 --> 00:07:22,533
Another idea is rocket debris or a meteor, but that cannot explain a craft that appears to follow a small aircraft for several minutes.

122
00:07:23,000 --> 00:07:30,934
A rough engine could also be fuel contamination or a mechanical fault, which would add stress to a young pilot.

123
00:07:30,934 --> 00:07:35,366
But a mechanical fault does not produce a long metallic shape.

124
00:07:35,833 --> 00:07:38,589
The disorientation theory explains a lot.

125
00:07:38,589 --> 00:07:42,488
It fits his low night experience, and the missing horizon.

126
00:07:42,488 --> 00:07:47,866
But it must also explain the green light, and the seventeen seconds of scraping.

127
00:07:48,333 --> 00:07:50,066
So we score it as partial.

128
00:07:50,066 --> 00:07:52,800
A strong candidate, but not a proven one.

129
00:07:53,266 --> 00:07:53,997
Test seven.

130
00:07:53,997 --> 00:07:56,188
The extraterrestrial explanation.

131
00:07:56,188 --> 00:08:01,500
A craft, intelligent, controlled, that played with the plane, then took it away.

132
00:08:01,966 --> 00:08:08,418
Researchers have catalogued many reports of unusual lights over Victoria around that time.

133
00:08:08,418 --> 00:08:13,866
But a crowd of reports is not a physical craft, and none was ever recovered.

134
00:08:14,333 --> 00:08:18,971
Nobody else on the ground or in the air reported the object near him.

135
00:08:18,971 --> 00:08:22,333
There is no radar track of it, and no photographs.

136
00:08:22,800 --> 00:08:28,886
And there is another thought, a darker and much more human one: that he chose to disappear.

137
00:08:28,886 --> 00:08:32,833
No evidence supports it, and his family always rejected it.

138
00:08:33,299 --> 00:08:36,495
No sign of him was ever reported ashore.

139
00:08:36,495 --> 00:08:37,454
No sighting.

140
00:08:37,454 --> 00:08:38,333
No contact.

141
00:08:38,333 --> 00:08:43,766
The staged disappearance idea, we score as unverified, and unlikely.

142
00:08:44,233 --> 00:08:49,378
The alien idea has one real piece of evidence: the words on the tape.

143
00:08:49,378 --> 00:08:55,866
But words are testimony, not proof, and a frightened pilot is an unreliable instrument.

144
00:08:56,333 --> 00:08:58,299
Time to bring the findings together.

145
00:08:58,766 --> 00:09:01,166
One more thing before the scorecard.

146
00:09:01,166 --> 00:09:04,766
Every report we have comes from one voice on one tape.

147
00:09:04,766 --> 00:09:10,433
That is why the transcript, and the seventeen seconds after it, carry so much weight.

148
00:09:10,899 --> 00:09:11,439
Debrief.

149
00:09:11,439 --> 00:09:13,666
Time to score the Valentich case.

150
00:09:14,133 --> 00:09:19,641
A real pilot, a real flight, a real recording, a real search: evidence.

151
00:09:19,641 --> 00:09:23,288
A report that could not find a cause: evidence.

152
00:09:23,288 --> 00:09:27,633
Low night experience and no horizon: real, and relevant.

153
00:09:28,100 --> 00:09:30,557
Spatial disorientation: partial.

154
00:09:30,557 --> 00:09:35,704
A craft with a green light that followed him: no physical evidence.

155
00:09:35,704 --> 00:09:38,469
A staged disappearance: no evidence.

156
00:09:38,469 --> 00:09:40,466
Alien abduction: unproven.

157
00:09:40,933 --> 00:09:41,776
The verdict.

158
00:09:41,776 --> 00:09:42,549
Unresolved.

159
00:09:42,549 --> 00:09:50,139
The most likely story is that a skilled but inexperienced young pilot lost his orientation over a black sea.

160
00:09:50,139 --> 00:09:55,200
But that does not explain everything, and nobody should pretend it does.

161
00:09:55,666 --> 00:10:00,766
Behind the mystery, there was a young man, with a family who never stopped wondering.

162
00:10:00,766 --> 00:10:03,466
Whatever happened out there, they lost a son.

163
00:10:03,933 --> 00:10:12,459
Next case: a place in Africa where nature seems to have switched on a nuclear reactor two billion years before we did.

164
00:10:12,459 --> 00:10:14,700
Natural, or ancient technology?

165
00:10:15,166 --> 00:10:18,824
What do you think happened in those last seventeen seconds?

166
00:10:18,824 --> 00:10:24,033
Tell us in the comments, and subscribe for the next ancient mystery put to the test.
